Context: on the surrounding pages Grothendieck works in a von Neumann algebra 𝒜 on a Hilbert space ℋ with a faithful normal semifinite trace T. He extends the Fuglede–Kadison determinant Δ, published in 1952 for finite factors, to operators of the form 1+A, with A in the trace-class ideal 𝔞 (roughly L¹(𝒜,T)). The previous page sets this up and shows 0 ≤ Δ(1+A) < ∞ for A ∈ 𝔞. It also introduces the reduced algebra 𝒜_E = E𝒜E, for a projection E, with the induced trace T_E and its determinant Δ_E.

This page:
(i) Localisation formula (3): Δ(1+A) = Δ_E(1_E + A) whenever A ∈ 𝒜_E. In words, the determinant of 1+A can be computed in any corner E𝒜E that contains A. On the orthogonal complement the operator is the identity and contributes the factor 1. Grothendieck calls this immediate ('comme on vérifie aussitôt').
(ii) Prop. 8, for A, B ∈ 1+𝔞:
- Δ(A) = Δ(A*);
- Δ(AB) = Δ(A)Δ(B);
- Δ(1) = 1.
The proof sketch reads 'Seule la deuxième formule est non triviale', so only multiplicativity needs work. He then reduces by a continuity argument to the case where there is a finite projection E (finite trace) with A, B ∈ 𝒜_E (up to the identity part). Formula (3) then turns the problem into one inside the finite algebra 𝒜_E. There the multiplicativity theorem of Fuglede–Kadison applies.
(iii) A crossed-out direct attempt (the large boxed area struck through with a wavy line) tries to prove multiplicativity from scratch:
- Δ(A)² = Δ(AA*) and Δ(A*)² = Δ(A*A);
- Δ(AB)² = Δ(B*A*AB) = Δ(B*HB) with H = A*A, followed by the queried step '=? Δ(A*A)Δ(B*B)';
- writing B*B = |B|² and putting L = |B|, he tries to replace Δ(B*HB) by Δ(LHL).
He abandoned this in favour of the reduction to the finite case.

Purpose: this builds a determinant theory on 1+L¹ in semifinite algebras. It leads, on the next page, to:
- the relative determinants Δ^{X,Y} between equivalent projections, with their multiplicativity (6);
- Prop. 9, a Ky-Fan-type variational formula Δ_A(t) = sup_{Tr E ≤ t} Δ_E(EAE), linked to rearrangements and generalized singular numbers.
